-- store_projection_toy.{mm0,mm1} — a *proved* toy model of the memory
-- projection stated in `mm0/store_projection.mm0`.
--
-- WHY THIS FILE EXISTS. `store_projection.mm0` states two claims (load and
-- store project onto the ISA model at width 8) and proves neither, because the
-- real proof needs a semantics for `jit_store_i64`/`jit_load_i64` and a
-- simulation between two state types. Those are not in this repo. What *can* be
-- settled today is the shape: pick concrete operations for the two sides and
-- check that the projection then goes through, from named and cited memory
-- axioms, with nothing assumed about the compiler.
--
-- WHAT IS PROVED HERE (25 theorems, 0 sorry):
--
--   toy_init_unset                a fresh memory reads `unset` at every address
--   toy_jit_store_visible         a store makes the value visible at the
--                                 written address
--   toy_isa_store_visible         the same for the model's definition at width 8
--   toy_store_projection          the two definitions agree at that address
--   toy_load_projection           the two load definitions return the same value
--   toy_jit_store_load_other      while a cell is free, a store leaves every
--   toy_isa_store_load_other      other address's content alone
--   toy_jit_two_cells_preserved   two stores at distinct addresses both stay
--   toy_isa_two_cells_preserved   readable: the second does not clobber the first
--   toy_jit_refresh_keeps_other_cell   a store at an address the memory
--   toy_isa_refresh_keeps_other_cell   already holds updates that cell in place
--                                 and leaves the other cell readable
--   toy_jit_refresh_visible       … and the refreshed address reads the *new*
--   toy_isa_refresh_visible       value back (no `vne` guard needed: what a
--                                 write returns at its own address does not
--                                 depend on the rest of the memory, while
--                                 whether the other cell survives does)
--   toy_jit_refresh_keeps_held_cells   … and leaves the other cell *held*
--   toy_jit_refresh_no_new_held    … and holds no address it did not hold
--   toy_jit_repeated_refresh_never_evicts  two refreshes at one address still
--                                 leave the other cell's value readable
--   toy_jit_third_store_drops_oldest   a third distinct store drops the earliest
--                                 address (capacity 2, FIFO)
--   toy_jit_fourth_store_drops_oldest  a fourth does the same to the next oldest
--   toy_jit_recent_cells_readable the two most recent stores both survive
--   toy_fourth_store_projection   the projection still holds on that deep memory
--   toy_jit_store32_visible       a width-4 store is visible at its address,
--                                 through the narrowing the width applies
--   toy_store32_projection        the two width-4 definitions agree there
--   toy_load32_projection         the two width-4 loads return the same value
--   toy_width4_load_after_wide_store    a width-4 load of a cell written at
--                                 width 8 returns the narrowed value
--   toy_width4_store_load_roundtrip     a width-4 store read back at width 4
--                                 returns the same narrowed value (idempotence)
--
-- THE CAPACITY IS THE POINT. The memory is **two cells**, and the axioms below
-- characterise it in the four cases a two-cell memory has. A write either hits
-- an address the memory already holds, or it does not:
--
--   refresh        the address is held, so the write replaces that cell's value
--                  and the other cell is untouched      (`at_put_held_other`,
--                  with `put_held` supplying "the address written is now held",
--                  and `put_held_other` "a cell survives a write elsewhere
--                  while one is free" — the fact that makes a refresh of an
--                  older cell reachable at all)
--   free cell      the address is not held and a cell is free, so nothing is
--                  evicted                             (`at_put_other`, guarded
--                  by `notfull`)
--   full, evicting the address is not held and no cell is free: after three
--                  distinct stores the *earliest* address reads `unset`
--                                                           (`at_drop`)
--   full, keeping  the newest store of the previous pair survives the next
--                  write, so the two latest stores are both readable
--                                                           (`at_keep_prev`)
--
-- `notfull` is deliberately **not** closed under `put`: `init_notfull` and
-- `put_init_notfull` say a fresh memory and the state after one store have a
-- free cell, and nothing says a further write preserves that. A general closure
-- axiom would be *unsound here* — with two cells, a write to a fresh address
-- must evict (`at_drop`), so "still has a free cell" cannot survive it. An
-- earlier revision of this file carried exactly that closure axiom; it was
-- dropped when the refresh case showed the two cannot both hold.
--
-- The axiom set is consistent, and the witness is short enough to state
-- informally: let `Mem` be a list of at most two `(address, value)` pairs, let
-- `put m a v` be `(a,v)` consed onto `m` with every pair at address `a` removed
-- and the result truncated to two, let `get m a` be the value of the first pair
-- whose address is `a` (else `unset`), let `held m a` mean some pair has
-- address `a`, let `notfull m` mean fewer than two pairs, `init` the empty
-- list, `veq` equality on `Val` and `vne` its negation. Then all thirteen
-- axioms hold: a refresh keeps the other pair, a write elsewhere into a one-pair
-- memory keeps it, three distinct writes leave exactly the last two (the first
-- of the three is gone), and after them the middle one is the older of the two
-- survivors. That is not a formal consistency proof — MM0 has no model-checking
-- entry point here — and this file does not claim one.
--
-- A SECOND WIDTH, AND WHAT IT CAN MEAN HERE. The real claim is about width 8,
-- so proving the same shape at another width tests that nothing in the
-- “two sides agree” argument is specific to one width. `Val` holds no bits, so a
-- narrower width cannot be a truncation of a stored bit pattern; it is modelled
-- as an uninterpreted `narrow4` applied to the value, with the two laws every
-- truncation obeys and nothing else: it is idempotent (`narrow4_idem`) and it
-- respects value equality (`narrow4_cong`). The width-4 load definitions narrow
-- on the way out, so the narrowing appears *in a proof* rather than only in a
-- name: a width-4 load of a cell written at width 8 returns `narrow4 v` by
-- congruence on `at_put`, and storing at width 4 then reading at width 4 returns
-- the same value because narrowing twice is narrowing once. What this does not
-- say is that `narrow4` is Cranelift's `i32` truncation or the ISA's 4-byte
-- write: those are still uninterpreted, exactly as `put`/`get` are.
--
-- WHAT IS *NOT* PROVED, and it is the whole distance to W9.3: that the emitted
-- Cranelift instruction computes `jit_store_i64`, that Sail's `vmem_write` at
-- width 8 computes `isa_store_w8`, that an aligned address stays in the linear
-- space, or that the fault branch is never taken there. Here `put`, `get`,
-- `held` and `veq` are uninterpreted and the two sides are *defined to be the
-- same term*, so the theorems say "if both sides are this operation, they
-- agree" — which is true, cheap, and exactly as strong as it looks. Nor does
-- the toy say anything about widths other than 8, or about how many cells the
-- real lowering keeps; the last of those is the gap the real obligation
-- inherits in a different form.
--
-- Keeping this file separate from `store_projection.mm0` is deliberate: that
-- artifact must contain no `axiom` and no `theorem` so it cannot quietly become
-- a proof of something it did not prove, and `scripts/verify-invariants`
-- enforces exactly that on it. Proofs live here, claims live there, and this
-- file cites the claim it models by digest.
--
-- theory coverage: used=put,get,init,unset,veq,vne,notfull,held unused=none
-- cites: mm0/store_projection.mm0 sha256=2b94039df2faccf5d4be102dfec17e2e72260271b64e6495c15249465ef86df7

provable sort Prop;
delimiter $ ( ) $;

-- ── The toy memory: two cells ────────────────────────────────────────────
-- `Mem` is a two-cell memory, `Val` is both address and value; both are
-- uninterpreted, so nothing here is 64-bit, aligned or linear. The cells are
-- not exposed as a constructor: `put`/`get`/`notfull`/`held` are the interface,
-- and what the two-cell capacity *means* is stated axiom by axiom rather than
-- derived from a data layout.
sort Val;
sort Mem;
-- The memory handed to the program: both cells free.
term init: Mem;
-- The content of an empty cell.
term unset: Val;
-- Narrowing a value to four bytes, uninterpreted: `Val` carries no bits, so this
-- is the *interface* of a narrower width rather than its implementation. The two
-- axioms below are the laws a truncation has, and they are what lets the width-4
-- theorems say something a renaming could not.
term narrow4 (x: Val): Val;
term put (m: Mem) (a v: Val): Mem;
term get (m: Mem) (a: Val): Val;
-- "At least one cell is still free", so the next write evicts nothing.
term notfull (m: Mem): Prop;
-- "The memory holds this address", so the next write to it is a refresh rather
-- than an append. Without it the model cannot tell the refresh case from the
-- eviction case: both write to an address, and only the state distinguishes
-- them.
term held (m: Mem) (a: Val): Prop;
-- Address inequality, uninterpreted like `veq`; the axioms below take it as a
-- hypothesis rather than deciding it.
term vne (x y: Val): Prop;

-- Value equality, as an uninterpreted predicate with the three facts the
-- proofs chain through. Stating these instead of importing an equality theory
-- is what keeps this file's coverage line honest: `veq` is the only equality
-- used, and it is assumed, not derived. `veq_trans` is what lets "read
-- unchanged" and "read-after-write" compose into "the earlier store survived".
term veq (x y: Val): Prop;
axiom veq_refl (x: Val): $ veq x x $;
axiom veq_trans (x y z: Val) (h1: $ veq x y $) (h2: $ veq y z $): $ veq x z $;
axiom vne_sym (x y: Val) (h: $ vne x y $): $ vne y x $;

-- The two laws of a narrowing: it is idempotent, and it takes equal values to
-- equal values. The second is what lets a proof about `get` reach a proof about
-- a narrowed `get` — without it the toy's equality is too weak to push anything
-- through `narrow4`, and the width-4 theorems below would be unprovable for a
-- reason that has nothing to do with memory.
axiom narrow4_cong (x y: Val) (h: $ veq x y $): $ veq (narrow4 x) (narrow4 y) $;
axiom narrow4_idem (x: Val): $ veq (narrow4 (narrow4 x)) (narrow4 x) $;

-- ── The toy memory's semantics: four cases, one axiom each ───────────────
-- Free cell: a fresh memory is empty, and the state after one store still has a
-- free cell — and stops there. See the header for why this is *not* closed
-- under `put`: closure would contradict `at_drop`, since in a full memory a
-- write to a fresh address evicts.
axiom get_init (a: Val): $ veq (get init a) unset $;
axiom init_notfull: $ notfull init $;
axiom put_init_notfull (a v: Val): $ notfull (put init a v) $;

-- Every write is visible at the address it writes, whether it refreshed a cell
-- or took a free one.
axiom at_put (m: Mem) (a v: Val): $ veq (get (put m a v) a) v $;

-- Refresh: the write's address is now held (so a second write there refreshes
-- again rather than appending a duplicate cell) …
axiom put_held (m: Mem) (a v: Val): $ held (put m a v) a $;
-- … and while a cell is free, a write elsewhere keeps the held cell. Unguarded
-- this would be false — a write to a fresh address in a full memory evicts the
-- oldest cell, which may be the held one.
axiom put_held_other (m: Mem) (a b v: Val) (h: $ vne a b $)
  (k: $ held m b $) (f: $ notfull m $):
  $ held (put m a v) b $;
-- The refresh itself: the written cell takes the new value (`at_put`) and the
-- other cell is untouched. This is the axiom that makes "a store to an address
-- the memory already holds disturbs nothing else" a proved statement instead of
-- an assumption hidden in the ordering of the two cells.
axiom at_put_held_other (m: Mem) (x y w: Val) (h: $ vne x y $)
  (k: $ held m x $):
  $ veq (get (put m x w) y) (get m y) $;

-- The held *set* is what a refresh leaves alone, and saying it in membership
-- terms is how this toy proves "a refresh never evicts" without arithmetic: the
-- written address is held afterwards (`put_held`), every other held address is
-- still held (`at_put_held_keeps`), and nothing becomes held
-- (`at_put_held_no_new`). Those three together say the set of held addresses —
-- hence its size — is unchanged by a refresh, which is the property a program
-- storing repeatedly to one address relies on.
--
-- `at_put_held_keeps` carries the guard `held m x` — the write must be a
-- refresh, because a write to a *fresh* address in a full memory evicts, and
-- `scripts/toy-witness` exhibits that memory. `at_put_held_no_new` needs no
-- guard beyond `vne x y`: a write introduces no held address other than the one
-- it writes, so a held address that is not the written one was already held. An
-- earlier draft guarded it with `held m x` too, and the witness checker rejected
-- the *guard* — the statement is true without it, so dropping it made the axiom
-- stronger and the rejection honest.
axiom at_put_held_keeps (m: Mem) (x y w: Val) (h: $ vne x y $)
  (j: $ held m y $) (k: $ held m x $):
  $ held (put m x w) y $;
axiom at_put_held_no_new (m: Mem) (x y w: Val) (h: $ vne x y $)
  (j: $ held (put m x w) y $):
  $ held m y $;

-- Free cell, writing elsewhere: nothing is evicted, so every other address
-- keeps its content. Guarded by `notfull`, for the same reason `put_notfull` is
-- not an axiom: an unguarded version would claim unbounded memory and
-- contradict `at_drop` below.
axiom at_put_other (m: Mem) (a b v: Val) (h: $ vne a b $) (k: $ notfull m $):
  $ veq (get (put m a v) b) (get m b) $;

-- Full, evicting: three consecutive stores at pairwise distinct addresses, and
-- the earliest of the three is gone — the capacity is two, oldest out first.
axiom at_drop (m: Mem) (x y z: Val) (vx vy vz: Val)
  (hxy: $ vne x y $) (hxz: $ vne x z $) (hyz: $ vne y z $):
  $ veq (get (put (put (put m x vx) y vy) z vz) x) unset $;

-- Full, keeping: the same three stores, and the middle one — the newest cell of
-- the previously full pair — is still readable. Without this the model would
-- say what is evicted but not what survives, which is the half a projection
-- proof actually needs.
axiom at_keep_prev (m: Mem) (x y z: Val) (vx vy vz: Val)
  (hxy: $ vne x y $) (hxz: $ vne x z $) (hyz: $ vne y z $):
  $ veq (get (put (put (put m x vx) y vy) z vz) y) vy $;

-- ── A second width-8 store, with the other refresh order ─────────────────
-- Every projection theorem below is true by construction, because both sides
-- are defined as the same `put`. That is the assumption this section makes
-- visible: `isa_store_w8_keep` is a store with the *other* refresh order — when
-- the written address is already held, the value the cell already has stays
-- (first write wins), where `put` keeps the newest. It is an uninterpreted
-- `term` rather than a `def`, because a different ordering is not expressible
-- as a composition of `put`; that is part of what the assumption is.
term isa_store_w8_keep (m: Mem) (a v: Val): Mem;

-- Its two observable laws. A refresh of a held cell changes nothing readable at
-- that address, and no other address is disturbed — a store that keeps the old
-- value adds no cell, so unlike `put` this needs no `notfull` guard.
axiom keep_at_put_held (m: Mem) (x w: Val) (k: $ held m x $):
  $ veq (get (isa_store_w8_keep m x w) x) (get m x) $;
axiom keep_at_put_other (m: Mem) (x y w: Val) (h: $ vne x y $):
  $ veq (get (isa_store_w8_keep m x w) y) (get m y) $;

-- ── The two sides of the projection, concretely ──────────────────────────
-- The emitted instruction's effect and the model's effect at width 8, both
-- defined as the same write. In the real projection these are *different*
-- definitions (Cranelift lowering vs. Sail `vmem_write`), and proving them equal
-- is the work this toy skips by construction.
def jit_store_i64 (m: Mem) (a v: Val): Mem = $ put m a v $;
def isa_store_w8 (m: Mem) (a v: Val): Mem = $ put m a v $;
def jit_load_i64 (m: Mem) (a: Val): Val = $ get m a $;
def isa_load_w8 (m: Mem) (a: Val): Val = $ get m a $;
-- The same four transformers one width down. The stores narrow as they write and
-- the loads narrow as they read, which is where a width can differ from another
-- width at all in a world without bit patterns.
def jit_store_i32 (m: Mem) (a v: Val): Mem = $ put m a (narrow4 v) $;
def isa_store_w4 (m: Mem) (a v: Val): Mem = $ put m a (narrow4 v) $;
def jit_load_i32 (m: Mem) (a: Val): Val = $ narrow4 (get m a) $;
def isa_load_w4 (m: Mem) (a: Val): Val = $ narrow4 (get m a) $;

-- ── What the model proves ────────────────────────────────────────────────
-- Note that these are theorems about the *definitions*, not restatements of
-- the axioms: the goals are written in terms of `jit_store_i64` /
-- `isa_store_w8` and the proofs go through only because the definitions unfold.
theorem toy_init_unset (a: Val):
  $ veq (get init a) unset $;

theorem toy_jit_store_visible (m: Mem) (a v: Val):
  $ veq (get (jit_store_i64 m a v) a) v $;

theorem toy_isa_store_visible (m: Mem) (a v: Val):
  $ veq (get (isa_store_w8 m a v) a) v $;

theorem toy_store_projection (m: Mem) (a v: Val):
  $ veq (get (jit_store_i64 m a v) a) (get (isa_store_w8 m a v) a) $;

theorem toy_load_projection (m: Mem) (a: Val):
  $ veq (jit_load_i64 m a) (isa_load_w8 m a) $;

-- Store, then load from an address other than the one written, while a cell is
-- free: the second cell holds the value the store did not touch, so the read is
-- unchanged. The hypothesis `vne a b` is the toy's stand-in for the real claim's
-- `aligned8`/linearity side conditions, and `notfull m` is the capacity side
-- condition — both are load-bearing.
theorem toy_jit_store_load_other (m: Mem) (a b v: Val)
  (h: $ vne a b $) (k: $ notfull m $):
  $ veq (get (jit_store_i64 m a v) b) (get m b) $;

theorem toy_isa_store_load_other (m: Mem) (a b v: Val)
  (h: $ vne a b $) (k: $ notfull m $):
  $ veq (get (isa_store_w8 m a v) b) (get m b) $;

-- Two stores at distinct addresses into a fresh memory: the first value is
-- still there, because the second write found a free cell.
theorem toy_jit_two_cells_preserved (a b va vb: Val) (h: $ vne a b $):
  $ veq (get (jit_store_i64 (jit_store_i64 init a va) b vb) a) va $;

theorem toy_isa_two_cells_preserved (a b va vb: Val) (h: $ vne a b $):
  $ veq (get (isa_store_w8 (isa_store_w8 init a va) b vb) a) va $;

-- Refresh: write `a`, write `b` (so both cells are taken), then write `a`
-- again. The second value is still what a read of `b` returns — the refresh
-- updated `a` in place instead of evicting `b`. This is the case an
-- evict-on-every-write model would get wrong, and the one a real program hits
-- whenever it increments a counter it has already stored.
theorem toy_jit_refresh_keeps_other_cell (a b va vb va2: Val)
  (h: $ vne a b $):
  $ veq (get (jit_store_i64 (jit_store_i64 (jit_store_i64 init a va) b vb) a va2) b) vb $;

theorem toy_isa_refresh_keeps_other_cell (a b va vb va2: Val)
  (h: $ vne a b $):
  $ veq (get (isa_store_w8 (isa_store_w8 (isa_store_w8 init a va) b vb) a va2) b) vb $;

-- The read half of the same case, and the one a program actually observes: after
-- the refresh, a load at the refreshed address returns the *new* value. Without
-- it the refresh case would say only that the write did no harm to the other
-- cell, which an implementation that dropped every write could also satisfy.
-- `at_put` alone gives it — a write is visible at the address it wrote — and
-- that is the point: the refresh is a write like any other for the address it
-- touches, and unlike one for every other address. There is **no `vne` guard
-- here**, while its companion theorem above needs one, and that asymmetry is
-- itself the content: which value you read back after a write does not depend on
-- how many cells the memory holds or whether the other cell is distinct, whereas
-- whether the *other* cell survives does.
theorem toy_jit_refresh_visible (a b va vb va2: Val):
  $ veq (get (jit_store_i64 (jit_store_i64 (jit_store_i64 init a va) b vb) a va2) a) va2 $;

theorem toy_isa_refresh_visible (a b va vb va2: Val):
  $ veq (get (isa_store_w8 (isa_store_w8 (isa_store_w8 init a va) b vb) a va2) a) va2 $;

-- The held-set half of the same case: the refresh leaves `b` held, and creates
-- no address that was not held before. Those are the two directions the size of
-- the held set could move in, which is why "the number of held addresses does
-- not change" is proved as membership rather than counted — the toy has no
-- arithmetic, and interval-free membership is what a real lowering would need
-- anyway to argue that a repeated store cannot push a live cell out.
theorem toy_jit_refresh_keeps_held_cells (a b va vb va2: Val)
  (h: $ vne a b $):
  $ held (jit_store_i64 (jit_store_i64 (jit_store_i64 init a va) b vb) a va2) b $;

theorem toy_jit_refresh_no_new_held (a b va vb va2: Val) (h: $ vne a b $)
  (k: $ held (jit_store_i64 (jit_store_i64 (jit_store_i64 init a va) b vb) a va2) b $):
  $ held (jit_store_i64 (jit_store_i64 init a va) b vb) b $;

-- And the consequence a program depends on: store `a`, store `b`, then refresh
-- `a` twice, and `b`'s value is still what a load returns. Nothing is evicted by
-- repetition at one address, however many times it repeats — the two-step case
-- is the induction step, and the axiom it rests on (`at_put_held_other`) is
-- stated for an arbitrary memory, so the step does not depend on how many
-- refreshes came before it.
theorem toy_jit_repeated_refresh_never_evicts (a b va vb va2 va3: Val)
  (h: $ vne a b $):
  $ veq (get (jit_store_i64 (jit_store_i64 (jit_store_i64 (jit_store_i64 init a va) b vb) a va2) a va3) b) vb $;

-- Capacity 2, oldest out first: the third distinct store drops the earliest
-- address. This is the eviction case, and it is an axiom instance rather than a
-- chain — nothing else in the toy has to be known for it.
theorem toy_jit_third_store_drops_oldest
  (a b c va vb vc: Val) (hab: $ vne a b $) (hac: $ vne a c $)
  (hbc: $ vne b c $):
  $ veq (get (jit_store_i64 (jit_store_i64 (jit_store_i64 init a va) b vb) c vc) a) unset $;

-- And the fourth store does it again, to the address that had become the
-- oldest: after `a`,`b`,`c`,`d` the memory holds `c` and `d`.
theorem toy_jit_fourth_store_drops_oldest
  (a b c d va vb vc vd: Val) (hbc: $ vne b c $) (hbd: $ vne b d $)
  (hcd: $ vne c d $):
  $ veq (get (jit_store_i64 (jit_store_i64 (jit_store_i64 (jit_store_i64 init a va) b vb) c vc) d vd) b) unset $;

-- The other half of the capacity: what the fourth store *kept* is still
-- readable. Eviction without this would leave the model unable to say that a
-- program can read back the value it just wrote after a burst of stores.
theorem toy_jit_recent_cells_readable
  (a b c d va vb vc vd: Val) (hbc: $ vne b c $) (hbd: $ vne b d $)
  (hcd: $ vne c d $):
  $ veq (get (jit_store_i64 (jit_store_i64 (jit_store_i64 (jit_store_i64 init a va) b vb) c vc) d vd) c) vc $;

-- … and the two sides still agree on that deep memory, so the capacity story
-- does not quietly break the projection it was added to support.
theorem toy_fourth_store_projection
  (a b c d va vb vc vd: Val):
  $ veq
      (get (jit_store_i64 (jit_store_i64 (jit_store_i64 (jit_store_i64 init a va) b vb) c vc) d vd) c)
      (get (isa_store_w8 (isa_store_w8 (isa_store_w8 (isa_store_w8 init a va) b vb) c vc) d vd) c) $;

-- ── The same projection one width down ────────────────────────────────────
-- Nothing above mentions a width in its *proof*, and these are the theorems that
-- say so in the artifact rather than in a comment. Note that the two width-4
-- sides are again defined as the same term — that is the toy's licence, and it is
-- the same licence the width-8 ones take.
theorem toy_jit_store32_visible (m: Mem) (a v: Val):
  $ veq (get (jit_store_i32 m a v) a) (narrow4 v) $;

theorem toy_store32_projection (m: Mem) (a v: Val):
  $ veq (get (jit_store_i32 m a v) a) (get (isa_store_w4 m a v) a) $;

theorem toy_load32_projection (m: Mem) (a: Val):
  $ veq (jit_load_i32 m a) (isa_load_w4 m a) $;

-- A width-4 read of a cell written at width 8 sees the narrowed value: `at_put`
-- gives the value, and congruence carries it through the narrowing the load
-- applies. This is the width difference showing up in a proof.
theorem toy_width4_load_after_wide_store (m: Mem) (a v: Val):
  $ veq (jit_load_i32 (jit_store_i64 m a v) a) (narrow4 v) $;

-- And a width-4 store read back at width 4 returns that same narrowed value,
-- because narrowing twice is narrowing once: congruence on `at_put` gives
-- `narrow4 (narrow4 v)`, and idempotence turns it into `narrow4 v`.
theorem toy_width4_store_load_roundtrip (m: Mem) (a v: Val):
  $ veq (jit_load_i32 (jit_store_i32 m a v) a) (narrow4 v) $;

-- ── Where the projection fails, once the two sides differ ─────────────────
-- The second definition read back at the address it wrote: nothing. This is
-- `keep_at_put_held` restated against the name a reader looks for, and it is
-- the mirror of `toy_jit_store_visible` — one store makes the new value visible
-- at its address, the other leaves the old one there.
theorem toy_keep_store_invisible (m: Mem) (a v: Val) (k: $ held m a $):
  $ veq (get (isa_store_w8_keep m a v) a) (get m a) $;

-- And where the projection actually fails: on the refresh trace of the case
-- above — store `a`, store `b`, refresh `a` — the JIT side reads the new value
-- (`toy_jit_refresh_visible` says `va2`), while this definition reads `va`: the
-- value from *before* the refresh. The two agree on every trace where only free
-- cells are taken, which is exactly why the assumption is easy to miss: the
-- toy's projection theorems never exercise a differing ordering.
theorem toy_keep_refresh_misses_the_write (a b va vb va2: Val)
  (h: $ vne a b $):
  $ veq (get (isa_store_w8_keep (jit_store_i64 (jit_store_i64 init a va) b vb) a va2) a) va $;
